1
00:00:04,450 --> 00:00:10,151
Imagine being surrounded by life. Millions of trees. Billions of insects.

2
00:00:10,191 --> 00:00:12,307
Rivers wider than cities.

3
00:00:13,407 --> 00:00:16,111
And still... being completely alone.

4
00:00:17,211 --> 00:00:22,003
In the Amazon rainforest, you can walk for days without seeing the sky.

5
00:00:22,043 --> 00:00:24,113
Every direction looks the same.

6
00:00:24,153 --> 00:00:29,626
And the trail you walked a minute ago can disappear behind you like water closing

7
00:00:29,666 --> 00:00:30,701
over a stone.

8
00:00:31,801 --> 00:00:33,702
This is the story of Noah.

9
00:00:33,742 --> 00:00:39,044
And of the sixteen days he spent lost in the largest forest on Earth.

10
00:00:48,644 --> 00:00:52,879
After the Arctic, Noah had promised himself something warmer.

11
00:00:53,979 --> 00:00:57,552
So when a botanical research team offered him a job,

12
00:00:57,592 --> 00:01:01,929
mapping weather sensors along a remote tributary of the Amazon,

13
00:01:01,969 --> 00:01:05,454
he said yes before they had finished explaining.

14
00:01:06,554 --> 00:01:11,459
Three days by boat from the nearest town. No roads. No phone signal.

15
00:01:11,499 --> 00:01:14,367
Just the river, brown and wide and slow,

16
00:01:14,407 --> 00:01:18,120
and the green walls of the forest on either side.

17
00:01:19,220 --> 00:01:21,503
Their guide was a man named Tomás.

18
00:01:21,543 --> 00:01:24,918
Forty-four years old, born in a riverside village,

19
00:01:24,958 --> 00:01:30,368
he had been guiding scientists through these forests since he was a teenager.

20
00:01:31,468 --> 00:01:35,305
First rule of the forest, Noah. Never leave the trail. Not for a photo.

21
00:01:35,345 --> 00:01:37,106
Not for a bird. Not for anything.

22
00:01:37,146 --> 00:01:40,736
The forest is very good at hiding people. Even from themselves.

23
00:01:41,836 --> 00:01:43,827
Never leave the trail. Got it.

24
00:01:44,927 --> 00:01:45,979
Everyone says got it.

25
00:01:46,019 --> 00:01:48,613
Then the forest shows them something beautiful.

26
00:01:49,713 --> 00:01:54,561
The research camp was a cluster of tents and hammocks under blue tarps,

27
00:01:54,601 --> 00:01:57,453
on a patch of high ground above the river.

28
00:01:57,493 --> 00:02:02,823
Six scientists, two boatmen, a generator that ran for two hours every evening,

29
00:02:02,863 --> 00:02:07,144
and more mosquitoes than Noah had ever imagined could exist.

30
00:02:08,244 --> 00:02:10,039
He shared a tent with Clara,

31
00:02:10,079 --> 00:02:15,281
a twenty-six-year-old botanist from São Paulo who had been coming to this forest

32
00:02:15,321 --> 00:02:16,854
since she was a student.

33
00:02:16,894 --> 00:02:20,976
She laughed when he asked whether the snakes were dangerous.

34
00:02:22,076 --> 00:02:27,635
That night, from his hammock at the research camp, Noah listened to the forest.

35
00:02:27,675 --> 00:02:28,698
Frogs. Insects.

36
00:02:28,738 --> 00:02:32,242
Strange calls he couldn't name. It wasn't silence.

37
00:02:32,282 --> 00:02:34,629
It was the opposite of silence.

38
00:02:39,729 --> 00:02:41,265
On the second morning,

39
00:02:41,305 --> 00:02:45,995
the team split into pairs to install sensors along a marked trail.

40
00:02:46,035 --> 00:02:50,484
Noah and a young botanist named Clara took the eastern loop.

41
00:02:51,584 --> 00:02:56,454
Around ten o'clock, Clara stopped to collect samples from a fallen tree.

42
00:02:56,494 --> 00:02:58,895
Noah wandered a few meters ahead.

43
00:02:59,995 --> 00:03:01,385
And then he heard it.

44
00:03:01,425 --> 00:03:04,314
A harsh, beautiful cry somewhere above him.

45
00:03:04,354 --> 00:03:07,433
A flash of red and blue between the leaves.

46
00:03:08,533 --> 00:03:13,609
A pair of scarlet macaws, landing on the branches of a giant kapok tree,

47
00:03:13,649 --> 00:03:17,422
just a little way off the trail. Just a little way.

48
00:03:18,522 --> 00:03:21,963
Just one photo. Twenty meters. I'll be right back.

49
00:03:23,063 --> 00:03:26,412
He took the photo. It was perfect. He took another.

50
00:03:26,452 --> 00:03:31,860
The macaws flew deeper into the forest, and he followed them for a few more steps.

51
00:03:31,900 --> 00:03:33,113
Then a few more.

52
00:03:34,213 --> 00:03:37,215
When he turned around, the trail was gone.

53
00:03:38,315 --> 00:03:40,112
Clara? Clara! Over here!

54
00:03:41,212 --> 00:03:44,709
The forest swallowed his voice after a few meters.

55
00:03:44,749 --> 00:03:48,388
Thick leaves, hanging vines, trunks wider than cars.

56
00:03:48,428 --> 00:03:52,682
Every direction looked exactly like every other direction.

57
00:03:53,782 --> 00:03:55,037
He checked his GPS.

58
00:03:55,077 --> 00:03:59,944
It was searching for satellites, blocked by a canopy forty meters thick.

59
00:03:59,984 --> 00:04:05,192
Then the screen flashed a message he would remember for the rest of his life.

60
00:04:05,232 --> 00:04:06,200
Battery low.

61
00:04:11,300 --> 00:04:13,886
The rain came twenty minutes later.

62
00:04:14,986 --> 00:04:17,135
Not a shower. A wall of water.

63
00:04:17,175 --> 00:04:23,190
In the Amazon, a single storm can drop more rain in an hour than some cities see in

64
00:04:23,230 --> 00:04:23,964
a month.

65
00:04:25,064 --> 00:04:26,725
The ground turned to mud.

66
00:04:26,765 --> 00:04:30,399
Small streams appeared from nowhere and became rivers.

67
00:04:30,439 --> 00:04:34,399
Thunder rolled through the trees like falling buildings.

68
00:04:35,499 --> 00:04:38,480
Somewhere close, with a crack like a gunshot,

69
00:04:38,520 --> 00:04:43,648
a giant tree gave way under the weight of the water and came down through the

70
00:04:43,688 --> 00:04:46,534
canopy, tearing vines and branches with it.

71
00:04:46,574 --> 00:04:48,817
The ground shook beneath his feet.

72
00:04:48,857 --> 00:04:51,893
It had fallen less than thirty meters away.

73
00:04:52,993 --> 00:04:57,756
Noah ran, searching for the trail, for the camp, for anything familiar.

74
00:04:57,796 --> 00:04:59,582
That was his first mistake.

75
00:04:59,622 --> 00:05:02,275
Running made him more lost, not less.

76
00:05:03,375 --> 00:05:06,760
When the storm finally passed, the light was fading.

77
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:10,045
He was soaked, scratched, and completely alone.

78
00:05:11,145 --> 00:05:15,017
Back at the trail, Clara had raised the alarm within the hour.

79
00:05:15,057 --> 00:05:18,045
Tomás led a search until it was too dark to see.

80
00:05:18,085 --> 00:05:22,462
They found one of Noah's footprints in the mud... and then nothing.

81
00:05:23,562 --> 00:05:25,459
Clara didn't sleep that night.

82
00:05:25,499 --> 00:05:29,914
She sat by the radio in the camp, replaying the moment over and over.

83
00:05:29,954 --> 00:05:33,982
She had looked down at a fallen tree for less than two minutes.

84
00:05:34,022 --> 00:05:36,238
When she looked up, he was gone.

85
00:05:37,338 --> 00:05:40,841
Thousands of kilometers away, in a small apartment,

86
00:05:40,881 --> 00:05:45,774
Noah's sister received a phone call from a number she didn't recognize.

87
00:05:45,814 --> 00:05:48,414
A search was underway, the voice said.

88
00:05:48,454 --> 00:05:50,897
There was no reason to panic yet.

89
00:05:55,997 --> 00:05:58,303
Night in the rainforest comes fast.

90
00:05:58,343 --> 00:06:00,447
One moment there is green light.

91
00:06:00,487 --> 00:06:02,916
The next, there is nothing at all.

92
00:06:04,016 --> 00:06:09,986
Noah remembered one thing Tomás had told him on the boat. Never sleep on the ground.

93
00:06:10,026 --> 00:06:14,827
Ants, snakes, scorpions, flood water. The ground belongs to them.

94
00:06:15,927 --> 00:06:20,488
He found two trees close together and wedged branches between them.

95
00:06:20,528 --> 00:06:23,440
A rough platform, half a meter off the mud.

96
00:06:23,480 --> 00:06:28,453
He covered it with giant palm leaves, the way he'd seen in documentaries,

97
00:06:28,493 --> 00:06:30,291
and hoped it would hold.

98
00:06:31,391 --> 00:06:34,195
And then the forest began to talk.

99
00:06:35,295 --> 00:06:36,175
Howls. Clicks.

100
00:06:36,215 --> 00:06:38,936
Something heavy moving through the leaves.

101
00:06:38,976 --> 00:06:42,287
A scream that might have been a bird, or might not.

102
00:06:42,327 --> 00:06:46,881
In the darkness, every sound was a predator. Every shadow had eyes.

103
00:06:47,981 --> 00:06:52,127
It's just frogs. It's just frogs. It's just... very large frogs.

104
00:06:53,227 --> 00:06:56,823
Then, around midnight, the trees began to glow.

105
00:06:57,923 --> 00:06:58,572
Fireflies.

106
00:06:58,612 --> 00:07:03,188
Thousands of them, blinking in slow green waves between the trunks.

107
00:07:03,228 --> 00:07:07,736
And on the forest floor, patches of fungus that shone with a soft,

108
00:07:07,776 --> 00:07:09,786
ghostly light of their own.

109
00:07:10,886 --> 00:07:15,669
Noah watched them until the fear wore off, and exhaustion took over.

110
00:07:15,709 --> 00:07:20,611
Lost in the most dangerous forest on Earth, he fell asleep smiling.

111
00:07:25,711 --> 00:07:29,603
It sounds strange, in a forest where it rains every day.

112
00:07:29,643 --> 00:07:34,707
But one of the greatest dangers of being lost in the Amazon is thirst.

113
00:07:35,807 --> 00:07:40,295
River water carries parasites and bacteria. Puddles are worse.

114
00:07:40,335 --> 00:07:44,530
Noah had a small water bottle and no way to boil anything.

115
00:07:44,570 --> 00:07:46,911
So he learned to collect rain.

116
00:07:48,011 --> 00:07:53,526
He folded large leaves into funnels and let them drip into his bottle during the

117
00:07:53,566 --> 00:07:56,304
afternoon storms. It took hours to fill.

118
00:07:56,344 --> 00:07:58,577
Every drop felt like treasure.

119
00:07:59,677 --> 00:08:04,452
He remembered something else from a survival video he'd watched years ago.

120
00:08:04,492 --> 00:08:07,640
Certain thick vines hold clean water inside them.

121
00:08:07,680 --> 00:08:09,918
He cut one with his pocket knife...

122
00:08:09,958 --> 00:08:13,491
and clear water poured out, cool and slightly sweet.

123
00:08:14,591 --> 00:08:17,174
Day three. Found water in a vine. A vine!

124
00:08:17,214 --> 00:08:21,394
I'm going to tell everyone about this. If I... when I get back.

125
00:08:22,494 --> 00:08:23,520
Food was harder.

126
00:08:23,560 --> 00:08:26,987
He knew almost nothing about which fruits were safe.

127
00:08:27,027 --> 00:08:29,786
Many of the brightest ones were poisonous.

128
00:08:29,826 --> 00:08:31,719
So he followed a simple rule.

129
00:08:31,759 --> 00:08:34,442
Only eat what you see the monkeys eat.

130
00:08:35,542 --> 00:08:40,773
On the fourth day, he found a tree covered in small yellow fruit, half eaten,

131
00:08:40,813 --> 00:08:43,922
with seeds scattered beneath it. He ate a few.

132
00:08:43,962 --> 00:08:46,166
They were sour, and wonderful.

133
00:08:47,266 --> 00:08:48,479
He wasn't starving.

134
00:08:48,519 --> 00:08:51,902
But he wasn't getting any closer to home, either.

135
00:08:53,002 --> 00:08:58,769
Twice, he heard the distant sound of a helicopter, somewhere far above the canopy.

136
00:08:58,809 --> 00:09:01,885
He screamed and waved until his voice broke.

137
00:09:01,925 --> 00:09:06,772
But from the air, the Amazon is a carpet of green forty meters thick.

138
00:09:06,812 --> 00:09:09,724
A man standing beneath it is invisible.

139
00:09:10,824 --> 00:09:12,652
The insects were relentless.

140
00:09:12,692 --> 00:09:16,189
Mosquitoes at dawn and dusk. Ants that bit like fire.

141
00:09:16,229 --> 00:09:21,260
On the fourth morning he found three leeches on his ankle, swollen and dark,

142
00:09:21,300 --> 00:09:25,454
and had to burn them off with the last flame of his lighter.

143
00:09:26,554 --> 00:09:28,267
He learned to move slowly.

144
00:09:28,307 --> 00:09:32,651
In the heat, every unnecessary step cost water he couldn't spare.

145
00:09:32,691 --> 00:09:36,697
He walked in the mornings, rested through the hottest hours,

146
00:09:36,737 --> 00:09:40,664
and built a new shelter every afternoon before the rain.

147
00:09:45,764 --> 00:09:49,867
From the first morning, Noah had done what he thought was smart.

148
00:09:49,907 --> 00:09:53,233
He picked a direction and walked in a straight line,

149
00:09:53,273 --> 00:09:58,541
carving an arrow into a tree trunk every few hundred meters so he would know where

150
00:09:58,581 --> 00:09:59,378
he'd been.

151
00:10:00,478 --> 00:10:02,506
On the fifth day, around noon,

152
00:10:02,546 --> 00:10:08,005
he stopped to rest against a huge trunk with roots like the fins of a rocket.

153
00:10:09,105 --> 00:10:14,110
And there, at the height of his shoulder, was an arrow. Carved with a pocket knife.

154
00:10:14,150 --> 00:10:16,367
And underneath it, the number one.

155
00:10:20,467 --> 00:10:23,384
It was his own mark. From the first morning.

156
00:10:23,424 --> 00:10:26,867
He had walked for four days... in a giant circle.

157
00:10:27,967 --> 00:10:32,259
It happens to almost everyone who is lost without landmarks.

158
00:10:32,299 --> 00:10:38,251
In experiments, people blindfolded in open fields, or walking through dense forest,

159
00:10:38,291 --> 00:10:41,067
drift in circles without ever noticing.

160
00:10:41,107 --> 00:10:43,160
One leg is slightly stronger.

161
00:10:43,200 --> 00:10:45,372
The brain fills in the rest.

162
00:10:46,472 --> 00:10:49,161
Four days. Four days for nothing. Nothing!

163
00:10:50,261 --> 00:10:54,700
He sat in the mud and, for a long time, he didn't move at all.

164
00:10:55,800 --> 00:10:58,839
Day five. I walked in a circle. For four days.

165
00:10:58,879 --> 00:11:03,983
I'm recording this so that if I ever feel smart again, I can listen to it.

166
00:11:05,083 --> 00:11:08,987
Then he remembered the second thing Tomás had told him.

167
00:11:09,027 --> 00:11:11,783
If you are ever lost, Noah, find water.

168
00:11:11,823 --> 00:11:16,132
Small water leads to big water. Big water leads to people.

169
00:11:21,232 --> 00:11:25,101
But to find water, he first needed to know where it was.

170
00:11:25,141 --> 00:11:29,550
And the only way to see anything in the Amazon is from above.

171
00:11:30,650 --> 00:11:34,943
The kapok tree beside him was the tallest thing for kilometers.

172
00:11:34,983 --> 00:11:39,809
Its trunk was covered in thick vines, like ropes left there for him.

173
00:11:40,909 --> 00:11:47,213
It took him almost two hours to climb thirty meters. His arms shook. His hands bled.

174
00:11:47,253 --> 00:11:49,065
Twice, he nearly fell.

175
00:11:50,165 --> 00:11:55,567
And then his head broke through the leaves, into open air and blinding sunlight.

176
00:11:56,667 --> 00:11:59,386
The forest stretched to every horizon.

177
00:11:59,426 --> 00:12:02,870
An ocean of green, rolling in waves of treetops.

178
00:12:02,910 --> 00:12:05,093
Clouds sat on it like ships.

179
00:12:06,193 --> 00:12:09,253
And there, to the south, between two hills...

180
00:12:09,293 --> 00:12:12,818
a thin silver line, glinting in the sun. A river.

181
00:12:13,918 --> 00:12:15,558
There you are. There you are.

182
00:12:16,658 --> 00:12:20,670
He memorized the line of the hills. The position of the sun.

183
00:12:20,710 --> 00:12:24,722
He climbed down with a plan for the first time in five days.

184
00:12:24,762 --> 00:12:30,449
Walk with the sun on your left in the morning. Toward the river. Toward big water.

185
00:12:35,549 --> 00:12:38,546
It took him two more days to reach the water.

186
00:12:38,586 --> 00:12:41,706
And when he did, it wasn't what he expected.

187
00:12:42,806 --> 00:12:47,251
In the rainy season, Amazon rivers rise by ten meters or more,

188
00:12:47,291 --> 00:12:50,144
spilling into the forest for kilometers.

189
00:12:50,184 --> 00:12:53,879
The locals call it the igapó. The flooded forest.

190
00:12:54,979 --> 00:12:59,240
Trees stood in black, mirror-still water up to their branches.

191
00:12:59,280 --> 00:13:01,390
Fish swam between their trunks.

192
00:13:01,430 --> 00:13:05,275
Somewhere out there, the real river channel was flowing.

193
00:13:05,315 --> 00:13:10,113
But between Noah and that channel lay kilometers of drowned forest.

194
00:13:11,213 --> 00:13:14,347
He waded in up to his waist, then his chest.

195
00:13:14,387 --> 00:13:16,366
The water was warm and dark.

196
00:13:16,406 --> 00:13:18,648
He couldn't see his own feet.

197
00:13:19,748 --> 00:13:23,835
People imagine piranhas tearing swimmers apart in seconds.

198
00:13:23,875 --> 00:13:25,756
The truth is less dramatic.

199
00:13:25,796 --> 00:13:31,519
Most piranhas are shy, and attacks on people are rare. But Noah didn't know that.

200
00:13:31,559 --> 00:13:35,907
Every brush of a fish against his legs made his heart stop.

201
00:13:37,007 --> 00:13:39,866
That evening, from a branch above the water,

202
00:13:39,906 --> 00:13:44,998
he saw two red lights gliding toward him across the surface. Then two more.

203
00:13:46,098 --> 00:13:48,439
Caimans. Cousins of the alligator.

204
00:13:48,479 --> 00:13:52,902
Their eyes reflected the last light of the sunset like coals.

205
00:13:54,002 --> 00:13:58,356
They circled the tree for a while, then drifted away into the dark.

206
00:13:58,396 --> 00:14:02,882
Noah spent the night wedged in the branches, too frightened to sleep,

207
00:14:02,922 --> 00:14:04,646
too tired to stay awake.

208
00:14:09,746 --> 00:14:15,277
He couldn't swim across kilometers of flooded forest. But maybe he could float.

209
00:14:16,377 --> 00:14:18,555
Balsa trees grow in the Amazon.

210
00:14:18,595 --> 00:14:24,492
Their wood is so light that a log as thick as a man can be carried on one shoulder.

211
00:14:24,532 --> 00:14:28,473
Noah found three fallen trunks near the water's edge.

212
00:14:29,573 --> 00:14:35,401
He tied them together with strips of vine and his own shoelaces. It took a full day.

213
00:14:35,441 --> 00:14:37,756
It looked terrible. It floated.

214
00:14:38,856 --> 00:14:40,459
Day nine. I built a boat.

215
00:14:40,499 --> 00:14:43,941
A very small, very bad boat. Tomás would laugh at it.

216
00:14:43,981 --> 00:14:46,497
I hope I get the chance to show him.

217
00:14:47,597 --> 00:14:51,784
He pushed off into the flooded trees, paddling with a broken branch,

218
00:14:51,824 --> 00:14:54,771
following the faint current toward the south.

219
00:14:55,871 --> 00:14:58,771
The first night on the raft was the worst.

220
00:14:58,811 --> 00:15:01,430
Every splash in the dark was a caiman.

221
00:15:01,470 --> 00:15:05,490
Every bump against a submerged branch was something worse.

222
00:15:05,530 --> 00:15:09,249
He didn't close his eyes until the sky turned grey.

223
00:15:10,349 --> 00:15:13,952
On the second day of paddling, the trees opened up...

224
00:15:13,992 --> 00:15:19,159
and he drifted out onto the river itself. Wide. Brown. Moving. Big water.

225
00:15:20,259 --> 00:15:22,184
Big water leads to people.

226
00:15:22,224 --> 00:15:25,398
He held on to that sentence like a rope.

227
00:15:26,498 --> 00:15:31,557
At night he tied the raft to overhanging branches near the bank and lay on his

228
00:15:31,597 --> 00:15:33,060
back, watching the sky.

229
00:15:33,100 --> 00:15:36,721
Without the canopy above him, the stars were everywhere.

230
00:15:36,761 --> 00:15:41,035
He picked out the ones his sister had taught him, a long time ago,

231
00:15:41,075 --> 00:15:43,578
on the roof of their parents' house.

232
00:15:44,678 --> 00:15:48,061
Twice, afternoon storms caught him in open water.

233
00:15:48,101 --> 00:15:51,415
The rain was so heavy he couldn't see the banks.

234
00:15:51,455 --> 00:15:57,054
He lay flat on the logs, held on with both hands, and let the river carry him.

235
00:15:58,154 --> 00:16:02,113
In the mornings, howler monkeys roared from the riverbanks,

236
00:16:02,153 --> 00:16:04,485
a sound like wind through a tunnel.

237
00:16:04,525 --> 00:16:09,296
River dolphins, pink and grey, surfaced beside the raft, looked at him,

238
00:16:09,336 --> 00:16:10,503
and dove again.

239
00:16:15,603 --> 00:16:19,730
On the eleventh day, the cut on his leg turned red and hot.

240
00:16:20,830 --> 00:16:25,765
He had scratched it on a thorn during the storm, on the very first day.

241
00:16:25,805 --> 00:16:28,779
In the heat and humidity of the rainforest,

242
00:16:28,819 --> 00:16:32,122
even a small wound can become dangerous fast.

243
00:16:33,222 --> 00:16:37,225
By the afternoon, he was shivering in thirty-degree heat.

244
00:16:37,265 --> 00:16:41,884
By night, the stars were moving in ways that stars do not move.

245
00:16:42,984 --> 00:16:46,433
Day twelve. Or thirteen. I'm not... I'm not sure.

246
00:16:46,473 --> 00:16:48,499
If anyone finds this phone...

247
00:16:48,539 --> 00:16:53,460
tell my sister I'm sorry I went so far away. Tell her I saw macaws.

248
00:16:54,560 --> 00:16:59,420
At one point he was certain he heard his sister's voice, calling him for dinner,

249
00:16:59,460 --> 00:17:04,565
the way she used to when they were kids. He answered her. He told her he was coming.

250
00:17:04,605 --> 00:17:06,715
He told her he was almost there.

251
00:17:07,815 --> 00:17:12,521
He drifted for two days, in and out of sleep, lying flat on the raft,

252
00:17:12,561 --> 00:17:13,828
too weak to paddle.

253
00:17:13,868 --> 00:17:16,374
He drank rain from his folded leaves.

254
00:17:16,414 --> 00:17:18,575
He talked to the river dolphins.

255
00:17:18,615 --> 00:17:21,310
He talked to Tomás, who wasn't there.

256
00:17:22,410 --> 00:17:26,598
And far upstream, the real Tomás hadn't stopped looking.

257
00:17:27,698 --> 00:17:30,532
The official search had ended after a week.

258
00:17:30,572 --> 00:17:32,536
The helicopters had gone home.

259
00:17:32,576 --> 00:17:38,083
But every morning, Tomás took his small motor canoe out onto a different stretch of

260
00:17:38,123 --> 00:17:39,888
river, calling Noah's name,

261
00:17:39,928 --> 00:17:45,357
and asking every fisherman he passed if they had seen a young foreigner, alone.

262
00:17:46,457 --> 00:17:50,489
Have you seen him? Tall. Dark hair. Grey backpack. Please. Tell everyone downstream.

263
00:17:55,589 --> 00:17:57,876
On the morning of the sixteenth day,

264
00:17:57,916 --> 00:18:02,399
a fisherwoman named Rosa was checking her nets at a bend in the river,

265
00:18:02,439 --> 00:18:05,433
with her grandson in the front of the canoe.

266
00:18:06,533 --> 00:18:07,900
The boy saw it first.

267
00:18:07,940 --> 00:18:12,387
A strange shape drifting in the current. Three logs, tied together.

268
00:18:12,427 --> 00:18:16,328
And on top of them, something that looked like a person.

269
00:18:17,428 --> 00:18:21,148
Oh my God. Hold the canoe steady. He's alive. He's alive!

270
00:18:22,248 --> 00:18:27,209
Rosa had heard about the lost foreigner from three different fishermen.

271
00:18:27,249 --> 00:18:29,041
Everyone on the river had.

272
00:18:29,081 --> 00:18:31,203
Tomás had made sure of that.

273
00:18:32,303 --> 00:18:36,287
My son was lost on the river once, many years ago. For three days.

274
00:18:36,327 --> 00:18:38,420
The whole village searched for him.

275
00:18:38,460 --> 00:18:42,573
When someone is lost on the river, the river belongs to everyone.

276
00:18:43,673 --> 00:18:47,477
They pulled Noah into the canoe, wrapped him in a blanket,

277
00:18:47,517 --> 00:18:49,664
and paddled him to their village.

278
00:18:49,704 --> 00:18:53,698
A cluster of wooden houses on stilts, above the waterline.

279
00:18:54,798 --> 00:19:00,133
Her grandson, Davi, eight years old, held the stranger's hand the whole way,

280
00:19:00,173 --> 00:19:04,730
and told him over and over, in a language Noah didn't understand,

281
00:19:04,770 --> 00:19:09,234
that everything was going to be okay. Noah understood anyway.

282
00:19:10,334 --> 00:19:15,250
Rosa cleaned his wound with boiled water and a paste of crushed leaves her

283
00:19:15,290 --> 00:19:17,594
grandmother had taught her to make.

284
00:19:17,634 --> 00:19:21,601
And someone with a radio called the next town downstream.

285
00:19:22,701 --> 00:19:26,540
Children from the village took turns sitting by his hammock,

286
00:19:26,580 --> 00:19:29,061
bringing him fish soup and fresh water,

287
00:19:29,101 --> 00:19:32,423
and asking him questions he was too tired to answer.

288
00:19:32,463 --> 00:19:35,785
Was it true he had walked in a circle for four days?

289
00:19:35,825 --> 00:19:39,466
Did he really sleep in a tree with caimans underneath?

290
00:19:40,566 --> 00:19:45,906
That evening, a motor canoe came racing around the bend, far too fast.

291
00:19:47,006 --> 00:19:49,155
I told you. Never leave the trail.

292
00:19:50,255 --> 00:19:52,829
The macaws were really beautiful, Tomás.

293
00:19:53,929 --> 00:19:54,782
They always are.

294
00:19:54,822 --> 00:19:56,757
That's why the forest uses them.

295
00:19:57,857 --> 00:20:00,572
Noah spent a week in hospital in Manaus,

296
00:20:00,612 --> 00:20:06,220
recovering from the infection and from severe dehydration. He had lost nine kilos.

297
00:20:06,260 --> 00:20:11,024
Doctors said that two more days on the river might have killed him.

298
00:20:12,124 --> 00:20:14,593
His sister flew in on the third day.

299
00:20:14,633 --> 00:20:17,311
She didn't say I told you so. Not once.

300
00:20:17,351 --> 00:20:21,891
She just held his hand, and didn't let go for a very long time.

301
00:20:22,991 --> 00:20:25,470
When Noah talks about the Amazon now,

302
00:20:25,510 --> 00:20:30,236
people expect stories about jaguars and piranhas and poisonous snakes.

303
00:20:30,276 --> 00:20:34,117
He tells them about vines full of water. About fireflies.

304
00:20:34,157 --> 00:20:39,617
About a fisherwoman and her grandson, and a guide who refused to stop looking.

305
00:20:40,717 --> 00:20:44,639
The forest didn't try to hurt him. The forest doesn't care.

306
00:20:44,679 --> 00:20:48,455
That's what makes it so dangerous... and so beautiful.

307
00:20:49,555 --> 00:20:50,603
But people care.

308
00:20:50,643 --> 00:20:54,055
And sometimes, that is enough to bring you home.

309
00:21:00,155 --> 00:21:03,063
If you were lost in the Amazon, what would you do?

310
00:21:03,103 --> 00:21:04,713
Stay where you are and wait?

311
00:21:04,753 --> 00:21:08,794
Or follow the water, and trust the river? Tell us in the comments.

312
00:21:09,894 --> 00:21:10,938
And remember...

313
00:21:10,978 --> 00:21:13,946
when everything ends, the story begins.
